</p></sec></body><back><app-group><app id="App1"><sec id="Sec21"><title>Extended data</title><p id="Par73">
<fig id="Fig7"><label>Extended Data Fig. 1</label><caption><title>PACs distribution drawn based on D18370 dendrites and F1877 axons.</title><p><bold>a</bold>. Spatial distribution of Potential Arbor Contacts (PACs) in the whole mouse brain. Colors scheme is the same as in Fig. <xref rid="Fig4" ref-type="fig">4a</xref>, indicating their soma locations. <bold>b</bold>. Heatmap of anatomical PACs distribution in whole mouse brain. Horizontal axis: target brain regions of PACs. Vertical axis: source brain regions of PACs. Color bar: the percentages of PACs (percent-normalized using the total PACs counts of each source region). <bold>c</bold>. Correlation between anatomical distribution of PACs and axonal projection across whole mouse brain. Scatter plot: the counts of PACs and PBs from the same regions.</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="41592_2025_2784_Fig7_ESM" id="d33e1672"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par74">
<fig id="Fig8"><label>Extended Data Fig. 2</label><caption><title>SEU-net: a mesoscale connectome with 148 brain regions including cortical layers.</title><p>Vertical axis: source brain regions. Horizontal axis: target brain regions. Color bar: regional connection strength (log10-transformed).</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="41592_2025_2784_Fig8_ESM" id="d33e1683"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par75">
<fig id="Fig9"><label>Extended Data Fig. 3</label><caption><title>Regional correlation PBs and PACs and a synaptome<sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR52">52</xref></sup>-based relative density of synapses.</title><p><bold>a</bold>. Correlation between synapse density and anatomical distributions of PBs with 102 shared brain regions. Colored dots: 102 common brain regions. <bold>b</bold>. Correlation between synapse density and anatomical distributions of PACs with 38 shared brain regions. Colored dots: 38 common brain regions. See Supplementary Table <xref rid="MOESM10" ref-type="media">8</xref> for the list of CCF region names showing up in <bold>a</bold> and <bold>b</bold>.</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="41592_2025_2784_Fig9_ESM" id="d33e1714"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par76">
<fig id="Fig10"><label>Extended Data Fig. 4</label><caption><title>Whole-brain region-wise gene co-expression map using the ABA dataset.</title><p>Vertical axis: 76 source brain regions. Horizontal axis: 76 target brain regions. Dot colors: regional connectivity of SEU-net. Color bar: regional connection strength from SEU-net. Dot sizes: gene co-expression scores.</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="41592_2025_2784_Fig10_ESM" id="d33e1725"/></fig>
</p><p id="Par77">
<fig id="Fig11"><label>Extended Data Fig. 5</label><caption><title>Whole-brain region-wise gene co-expression map using a MERFISH dataset.</title><p>Vertical axis: 76 source brain regions. Horizontal axis: 76 target brain regions. Dot colors: regional connectivity of SEU-net. Color bar: regional connection strength from SEU-net. Dot sizes: gene co-expression scores.</p></caption><graphic position="anchor" xlink:href="41592_2025_2784_Fig11_ESM" id="d33e1736"/></fig>
</p></sec></app></app-group><fn-group><fn><p><bold>Publisher&#x02019;s note</bold> Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations.</p></fn><fn><p>These authors contributed equally: Feng Xiong, Hanchuan Peng.</p></fn></fn-group><sec><sec id="FPar1"><title>Extended data</title><p id="Par69">is available for this paper at 10.1038/s41592-025-02784-2.</p></sec><sec id="FPar2" sec-type="supplementary-material"><title>Supplementary information</title><p id="Par70">The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1038/s41592-025-02784-2.</p></sec></sec><ack><title>Acknowledgements</title><p>This project was mainly supported by several grants awarded to H.P. H.P. is a New Cornerstone Investigator and a SANS Senior Investigator. L.L. was also supported by a MOST (China) Brain Research Project, &#x02018;Mammalian Whole Brain Mesoscopic Stereotaxic 3D Atlas&#x02019; (project grant numbers 2022ZD0205200 and 2022ZD0205204). The funders had no role in study design, data collection and analysis, decision to publish or preparation of the paper. We thank P. Qian, Y. Wang and Z. Zhao for the discussions and comments on the paper. We also thank Y. Liu, Z. Yun and S. Jiang, members of H.P.&#x02019;s New Cornerstone Science Laboratory, for providing supporting data.</p></ack><notes notes-type="author-contribution"><title>Author contributions</title><p>H.P. conceptualized and designed this study, and managed the entire project. F.X. and L.L. generated the analysis results along with biological interpretation under the detailed instruction of H.P. H.P. wrote the paper with input from all coauthors.</p></notes><notes notes-type="peer-review"><title>Peer review</title><sec id="FPar3"><title>Peer review information</title><p id="Par71"><italic>Nature Methods</italic> thanks Guoqiang Bi, Michael Reimann and the other, anonymous, reviewer(s) for their contribution to the peer review of this work. Primary Handling Editor: Nina Vogt, in collaboration with the <italic>Nature Methods</italic> team.</p></sec></notes><notes notes-type="data-availability"><title>Data availability</title><p>The dataset includes 1,877 fully reconstructed neurons with predicted axonal boutons, as well as dendritic reconstructions of 18,370 neurons. All data are publicly available via Zenodo at 10.5281/zenodo.10677899 (ref. <sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR63">63</xref></sup>). The generated connectomes and results are also archived in the repository. Connection data for Allen-net are available at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://connectivity.brain-map.org/">https://connectivity.brain-map.org/</ext-link>. BRICseq-net connectivity data were obtained from ref. <sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR51">51</xref></sup>. The ABA dataset is available at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://mouse.brain-map.org/">https://mouse.brain-map.org/</ext-link>. The MERFISH dataset is available via the portal at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://portal.brain-map.org/atlases-and-data/bkp/abc-atlas">https://portal.brain-map.org/atlases-and-data/bkp/abc-atlas</ext-link>. The synaptome data can be accessed at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.brain-synaptome.org">www.brain-synaptome.org</ext-link>.</p></notes><notes notes-type="data-availability"><title>Code availability</title><p>All codes for data generation and statistical analysis are publicly accessible via Zenodo at 10.5281/zenodo.10677899 (ref. <sup><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR63">63</xref></sup>). The codes and scripts are released under the MIT License at <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT">https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT</ext-link>.</p></notes><notes id="FPar4" notes-type="COI-statement"><title>Competing interests</title><p id="Par72">The authors declare no competing interests.</p></notes><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>References</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><citation-alternatives><element-citation id="ec-CR1" publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sporns</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Tononi</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>K&#x000f6;tter</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The human connectome: a structural description of the human brain</article-title><source>PLoS Comput. Biol.</source><year>2005</year><volume>1</volume><fpage>e42</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pcbi.0010042</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16201007</pub-id>
</element-citation><mixed-citation id="mc-CR1" publication-type="journal">Sporns, O., Tononi, G. &#x00026; K&#x000f6;tter, R. The human connectome: a structural description of the human brain. <italic>PLoS Comput. Biol.</italic><bold>1</bold>, e42 (2005).<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16201007</pub-id>
